Job 27 – Read a Production Log
Title
Read a Production Log using File Handling
Aim
To learn how to read a production log file in Python and understand different file modes such as r, w, a, and x.
1. Introduction
A file is used to store data permanently. In production engineering, files can store production records, machine readings, inspection data, and work logs.
Python provides the open() function to work with files.
Syntax
open("filename", "mode")
The with open() statement is preferred because it automatically closes the file after the work is completed.
2. Reading a Production Log
A production log may contain records like:
P01,25.5,A
P02,30.2,B
P03,28.7,A
Program
with open("production_log.txt", "r") as f:
    for line in f:
        line = line.strip()

        if not line:
            continue

        code, dia, shift = line.split(",")

        print(code.strip(), float(dia))
Explanation
open() opens the file.
"r" means read mode.
with automatically closes the file.
for line in f reads the file one line at a time.
strip() removes extra spaces and newline characters.
continue skips blank lines.
split(",") separates the values.
float(dia) converts the diameter into a decimal number.
3. Three Ways to Read a File
for line in f
Reads the file one line at a time.
for line in f:
    print(line)
Best for: Large files.
readlines()
Reads all lines and stores them in a list.
lines = f.readlines()
print(lines)
Best for: Small files.
read()
Reads the complete file as one string.
data = f.read()
print(data)
Best for: Reading the complete contents at once.
Four File Modes
Python provides four main file modes for handling files. The r (read) mode is used to read data from an existing file. 
The w (write) mode is used to write new data, but it clears the old content of the file when it is opened. 
The a (append) mode keeps the existing content and adds new data at the end of the file. 
The x (create) mode is used to create a new file and gives an error if the file already exists.
Examples
Read:
open("log.txt", "r")
Write:
open("log.txt", "w")
Append:
open("log.txt", "a")
Create:
open("log.txt", "x")
5. Important Rule
The w mode is dangerous for existing files because it clears the file as soon as the file is opened.
For example:
with open("log.txt", "w") as f:
    f.write("New Record")
The previous data will be removed.
If we want to add a new record without removing old records, use a mode:
with open("log.txt", "a") as f:
    f.write("P04,32.5,B\n")
6. Output
For the given production log, the program displays values such as:
P01 25.5
P02 30.2
P03 28.7
